% The portable regex subset. Patterns are usually written as raw 'literals' so
% backslashes need no doubling.

### name: re.match
--- source
RMATCH('^\d{3}$', "123")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.match.false
--- source
RMATCH('^\d{3}$', "12x")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.match.anchors-whole-subject
--- note
PCRE's $ also matches before a trailing newline unless the D modifier is set;
SEL anchors to the very end in both hosts.
--- source
RMATCH('^abc$', "abc\n")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.dot-matches-newline
--- note
Dotall is permanently on, so `.` means "any code point" in both engines.
--- source
RMATCH('^a.b$', "a\nb")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.dot-counts-code-points
--- source
RMATCH('^.$', "👍")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.digit-class-is-ascii
--- note
Neither engine makes \d Unicode-aware under the u flag; an Arabic-Indic digit
must not match.
--- source
RMATCH('^\d$', "\u{0661}")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.find
--- source
RFIND('ż', "Zażółć")
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: re.find.code-point-offsets
--- note
PHP reports byte offsets and JS reports UTF-16 offsets; SEL reports neither.
--- source
RFIND('a', "👍ża")
--- expect
num 3
===
### name: re.find.not-found
--- source
RFIND('q', "abc")
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: re.groups
--- source
RGROUPS('(\d+)-(\d+)', "10-20")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"10-20", "2"=t"10", "3"=t"20"}
===
### name: re.groups.no-match
--- source
RGROUPS('(z)', "abc")
--- expect
none
===
### name: re.groups.non-participating
--- source
RGROUPS('(a)|(b)', "a")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"a", "2"=t"a", "3"=t""}
===
### name: re.replace
--- source
RREPLACE('(a)(b)', "$2$1", "abab")
--- expect
text "baba"
===
### name: re.replace.whole-match
--- source
RREPLACE('\d+', "[$0]", "a12b3")
--- expect
text "a[12]b[3]"
===
### name: re.replace.literal-dollar
--- source
RREPLACE('a', "$$", "a")
--- expect
text "$"
===
### name: re.replace.js-specials-are-literal
--- note
JS would treat $& as the whole match; SEL understands $0-$9 and $$ only.
--- source
RREPLACE('a', "x$&y", "a")
--- expect
text "x$&y"
===
### name: re.replace.zero-width
--- source
RREPLACE('x*', "-", "ab")
--- expect
text "-a-b-"
===
### name: re.flag.i
--- source
RMATCH('^a+$', "AAA", "i")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.flag.i.rejects-non-ascii-pattern
--- source
RMATCH('ą', "Ą", "i")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG
===
### name: re.flag.m-not-offered
--- source
RMATCH('^a$', "a", "m")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG
===
### name: re.flag.unknown
--- source
RMATCH('a', "a", "x")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG
===

% Everything below is rejected at compile time rather than being allowed to mean
% different things in PCRE and ECMAScript.

### name: re.reject.lookahead
--- source
RMATCH('(?=a)', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.lookbehind
--- source
RMATCH('(?<=a)b', "ab")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.posix-class
--- source
RMATCH('[[:alpha:]]', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.unicode-property
--- source
RMATCH('\p{L}', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.backreference
--- source
RMATCH('(a)\1', "aa")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.possessive
--- source
RMATCH('a++', "aa")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.atomic-group
--- source
RMATCH('(?>a)', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.inline-modifier
--- source
RMATCH('(?i)a', "A")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.anchor-escape
--- source
RMATCH('\Aa', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.unsupported-escape
--- source
RMATCH('\xff', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.bare-brace
--- source
RMATCH('a{b', 'a{b')
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.accept.escaped-brace
--- source
RMATCH('^a\{b$', 'a{b')
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.accept.non-capturing-group
--- source
RMATCH('^(?:ab)+$', "abab")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.accept.lazy-quantifier
--- source
RREPLACE('<.+?>', "-", "<a><b>")
--- expect
text "--"
===
### name: re.accept.alternation-and-class
--- source
RMATCH('^[a-c]+|z$', "abc")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.reject.word-boundary
--- note
PHP's u modifier enables PCRE2's UCP, so `é` is a word character there and is not
in ECMAScript. A word boundary therefore cannot be made portable and is refused
rather than allowed to differ between backend and frontend.
--- source
RFIND('\bb', "a b")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.vertical-tab-escape
--- note
PCRE reads \v as any vertical whitespace; ECMAScript reads it as U+000B.
--- source
RMATCH('a\vb', "a\u{0B}b")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.negated-escape-in-class
--- source
RMATCH('[\D]', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.reject.leading-bracket-in-class
--- note
PCRE reads a leading ] as a literal; ECMAScript reads [] as an empty class.
--- source
RMATCH('[]]', "]")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.accept.escaped-bracket-in-class
--- source
RMATCH('^[\]]$', "]")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.word-class-is-ascii
--- note
Under PHP's UCP, an unrewritten \w would match an accented letter.
--- source
RMATCH('^\w$', "é")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.space-class-is-ascii
--- note
Both engines' native \s match U+00A0; SEL's expanded ASCII form does not.
--- source
RMATCH('^\s$', "\u{00A0}")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.class-escape-inside-class
--- source
RMATCH('^[\d.]+$', "3.14")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.negated-class-escape
--- source
RMATCH('^\D+$', "abc")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.realistic.postcode
--- setup
CODE = "31-874"
--- source
RMATCH('^\d{2}-\d{3}$', CODE)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.icase.folds-kelvin-sign
--- note
The i flag uses *simple* case folding, the same as ECMAScript's `u` mode and
PCRE2's `ui`. Exactly two non-ASCII code points fold to an ASCII letter —
U+212A KELVIN SIGN to "k" and U+017F LATIN SMALL LETTER LONG S to "s" — and an
implementation whose host folds only ASCII will silently disagree here. That is
the whole reason these cases exist: the pattern is ASCII, so `i` is permitted,
but the *subject* still reaches the engine's folding rules.
--- source
RMATCH('^k$', "\u{212A}", "i")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.icase.folds-long-s
--- source
RMATCH('^s$', "\u{017F}", "i")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: re.icase.no-full-folding
--- note
Full case folding would make U+00DF match "ss". Simple folding does not, and
simple folding is what all hosts must do.
--- source
RMATCH('^ss$', "\u{00DF}", "i")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.icase.dotted-capital-i-does-not-fold
--- note
U+0130 has only a full and a Turkic folding, no simple one, so it does not match
"i" even under the i flag.
--- source
RMATCH('^i$', "\u{0130}", "i")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.icase.angstrom-does-not-fold-to-ascii
--- source
RMATCH('^a$', "\u{212B}", "i")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.icase.position-is-the-original-subject
--- source
RFIND('k', "x\u{212A}y", "i")
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: re.icase.groups-return-the-original-text
--- note
The match is found by folding, but what comes back is the subject's own
characters — not the folded form used to find it.
--- source
RGROUPS('(k)', "\u{212A}", "i")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"K", "2"=t"K"}
===
### name: re.icase.replace-splices-the-original
--- source
RREPLACE('k', "-", "a\u{212A}b", "i")
--- expect
text "a-b"
===
### name: re.replace.caret-matches-only-at-the-start
--- note
RREPLACE walks to each subsequent match by continuing the scan from an offset.
`^` must still mean "the start of the subject", not "the start of this scan" —
cl-ppcre binds \A to the scan offset, which made this replace every character.
--- source
RREPLACE('^a', "X", "aaa")
--- expect
text "Xaa"
===
### name: re.replace.empty-caret-matches-once
--- source
RREPLACE('^', "X", "abc")
--- expect
text "Xabc"
===
### name: re.replace.caret-inside-alternation
--- source
RREPLACE('(^|x)a', "[$1]", "aaa")
--- expect
text "[]aa"
===
### name: re.replace.caret-alternation-tail
--- source
RREPLACE('a|^', "X", "bab")
--- expect
text "XbXb"
===
### name: re.quantifier.reversed-bounds-rejected
--- note
{2,1} is empty and every engine but cl-ppcre rejects it outright, so the subset
checker has to reject it explicitly rather than delegating.
--- source
RMATCH('a{2,1}', "aa")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.quantifier.reversed-bounds-rejected-in-rfind
--- source
RFIND('a{3,1}', "aa")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.quantifier.bound-at-the-cap
--- note
65535 is PCRE2's own hard limit rather than a number SEL chose; above it PCRE
refuses to compile at all, so no larger cap could be honoured on a PHP host.
--- source
RMATCH('a{65535}', "a")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: re.quantifier.bound-beyond-the-cap
--- source
RMATCH('a{65536}', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
### name: re.quantifier.enormous-bound
--- note
ECMAScript and cl-ppcre accept this and simply never match; PCRE2 and SRELL
reject it. The subset checker settles it rather than delegating.
--- source
RMATCH('a{99999999999}', "a")
--- expect
error E_REGEX_SYNTAX
===
